#!/bin/sh
#
# script/names: who named this, when, and what was refused (milestone 115).
#
# PROVISIONAL NAME. This script is one day old and its own name has not been ratified; see the
# `Name:` block at the end of this comment.
#
# Why this exists. A lane proposed `system_builder` for the crate milestone 96 extracted, the
# maintainer endorsed it, and calef overruled it to `system_initializer`. Only afterwards did anyone
# find that milestone 63 had **already refused `system_builder`**, and for a reason nobody had
# located: `components/src/builder.rs` called itself "a minimal init: the system builder" then, so
# two programs would claim one phrase. The refusal existed, in one table cell inside one milestone block,
# invisible at the moment it was needed. A blind rename then swept the old name out of that very
# row, and the record of the refusal was nearly destroyed by the rename it should have prevented.
#
# **The refusals are the valuable half.** A ratified name is visible in the tree, because it is the
# name; a refused one is visible nowhere, and the person who most needs it is the person about to
# propose it again.
#
# Why a query and not a table. The first draft of milestone 115 proposed one ratified-names table in
# design/naming.md. calef rejected it on 2026-08-04 for scaling the way the original DECISIONS.md and
# design/roadmap.md scaled, and size is the smaller half of that: every lane that adds a name would
# edit **one file**, which is exactly what produced three section-number collisions in a day. So the
# provenance lives **at the name**, in the header of the crate, program or script it belongs to,
# where adding a name touches one file and two lanes naming two things cannot collide. This script
# derives the table. Same family as script/roadmap, script/decisions and script/catch-up: a computed
# report over the tree, which therefore cannot drift from it.
#
# What carries a provenance block, and where. **Four kinds, not the three this list held until
# 2026-08-18**, and the fourth is the one that matters for keeping the list honest, because it is
# discovered rather than enumerated:
#
#   crates/<name>/src/lib.rs   the crate's module doc  (`//! Name: ...`)
#   components/src/<name>.rs   the program's module doc (`//! Name: ...`)
#   fixtures/src/<name>.rs     the same, for a test or benchmark program
#   script/<name>              the entry point's comment block (`# Name: ...`)
#   <anywhere>/Cargo.toml      a Cargo package outside crates/ (`# Name: ...`), found by walking
#                              the tree, so a package arriving tomorrow is covered without an edit
#                              here: kernel, xtask, redoxfs_server, tools/redoxfs_host, and the
#                              root-level workspaces std_exerciser and entropy_backend
#
# And four kinds added 2026-09-24, when calef asked that provisional names queue for him rather
# than reach him one at a time. They are many names per file, so they are found by marker:
#
#   item       any `.rs` file: a `///` doc run holding a `/// Name: ...` paragraph, for the function,
#              constant, type, module, macro, field or variant it documents, whatever its visibility
#   module     any `.rs` file that is not a crate root or program: its `//! Name: ...` block
#   directory  a directory of `.md` under design/, notes/ or briefs/: a paragraph of its README.md
#              opening `Name:` (emphasis allowed, bold not), which §75 already required
#   document   each `.md` stem in such a directory: a paragraph of the document itself opening
#              `Name:`, written under its title (calef, 2026-10-24 UTC), or the directory's block
#              when it has none. A README paragraph opening `` `stem` Name: `` is still read until
#              helpers/document_name_migrate.py has moved the last one; `--check` counts them
#
# An item or module WITHOUT a marker is not tracked and never fails: thousands exist and nothing
# says why they are named so. A lane minting one writes the marker, which is how it reaches the
# worklist. A bold `**Provisional name**` opening a doc line is the one shape that fails, because it
# reads as a record to a person and is invisible here. Stems that open with a digit (numbered or
# dated records) and everything under design/roadmap/ (milestone slugs, which calef ruled drafts on
# 2026-08-04) are skipped.
#
# The form, which is all that is checked:
#
#   Name: ratified <YYYY-MM-DD> (<who>, <where>). Refused `x` (why), `y` (why).
#   Name: recorded (<where>). <what the history says, and what it does not settle>
#   Name: unrecorded. <what the history does and does not say>
#
# A block runs from its `Name:` line to the END OF ITS COMMENT RUN, across paragraph breaks, so it
# may wrap and may argue one refusal per paragraph. `Refused` is optional and every refused name is
# in backticks, which is what makes `script/names <name>` able to answer "has this been proposed
# before" from the refusals as well as from the tree.
#
# **It used to stop at the first empty comment line**, and that sentence is what hid 91 refusals:
# a block written one argument per paragraph put all of them out of reach, and nobody noticed for as
# long as the convention had existed. Changed 2026-09-18; `helpers/name_provenance.py` carries the
# measurement and what the wider read still gets wrong. A block is now expected to be the LAST thing
# in its comment, which 139 of 143 files already did.
#
# **Three states, because two of them were one job wearing one word.** The first cut of this script
# had `ratified` and `unrecorded`, and the second answered two different questions at once. "Nobody
# in this tree can say why this is called that" needs research *and* a ruling; "here is the argument,
# nobody ever signed it" needs only the ruling. Those are different amounts of calef's time, and a
# worklist that cannot tell them apart is a list rather than a plan. So:
#
#   ratified    calef ruled, with the date and what was refused.
#   recorded    the tree argues the name somewhere (a milestone block, a decision, a header) and
#               nobody ever put it to him.
#   unrecorded  nothing outside this block says why the name is what it is.
#
# `recorded` carries a citation for the same reason `ratified` carries a date: the claim is that the
# reasoning exists somewhere, so a block that will not say where has not made it. That is checked as
# form, never as truth.
#
# **A `Name:` block is never its own evidence.** Every one of these was written by milestone 115, so
# reading them as "the tree explains this name" would make the record prove itself and every name
# `recorded` by construction. The citation points somewhere else, and that is the whole content of
# the distinction.
#
# **`unrecorded` is a first-class answer and must stay one.** Most of this tree's vocabulary arrived
# before anyone was writing naming decisions down, and inventing a ratification to fill a row would
# put a false claim in the one record whose entire job is saying who claimed what. Where the history
# does not say, the entry says it does not say, and cites the commit that introduced the name so the
# next reader starts where this one stopped.
#
#   script/names              the table, by kind
#   script/names --unratified the worklist: what still wants an architect, in the order to work it;
#                             items and modules another Cargo package reaches come first
#   script/names --refused    every refused name, and what holds the refusal
#   script/names --unrecorded the names nobody wrote a reason for
#   script/names <name>       one name: its provenance, or the entry that refused it
#   script/names --check      every surface carries a block; non-zero on a problem (script/lint)
#
# **The gate is presence, never `ratified`.** The one exception is a name calef has already ruled:
# `--check` fails it after 45 days unrenamed (helpers/name_ruled.py). Nothing else fails a build
# because a name is waiting on calef, and it must stay that way: a lint that demanded the queue be drained would block every
# unrelated merge behind a review nobody can hurry, which is the wall this milestone exists to avoid
# building. The worklist is how the queue gets worked; the gate only insists that a name says which
# state it is in. There are **four** states rather than the three listed above:
# §89 (`provisional` becomes the fourth provenance state) added `provisional` on 2026-08-16, a
# claim about INTENT where the other three are claims about the RECORD, and it is the largest of the
# four today.
#
# BUGS. It checks that a name carries provenance and that the provenance parses. It cannot check
# that the recorded reason is **still true**, that a date is right, that a `recorded` citation leads
# anywhere, or that an `unrecorded` entry is honest rather than lazy. That is the same limit
# script/decisions records for `§N` citations, and it is not closeable by a script: a reason is
# prose, and prose is checked by reading.
#
# Name: provisional, and it is this script's own three-state vocabulary applied to itself. Minted
# by milestone 115's lane on 2026-08-04. `provenance` was considered and set aside as naming the
# mechanism rather than the subject; `ratifications` names half the record and drops the refusals,
# which are the half that pays. DECISIONS §134 has since cited this script by name as an
# established member of the `script/` family's reporting half, which signs the form and leaves the
# stem where it was: 134 was choosing between `falsifications` and `falsify`, not between `names`
# and `provenance`. no architect has ratified it.

set -e

ROOT="$(CDPATH='' cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")/.." && pwd)"

exec python3 - "$ROOT" "${1:-}" <<'PY'
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys

root, mode = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]

# The enumeration and the `Name:` block parse this script shares with `script/metrics`, in one place
# rather than copied into both, by milestone 236 (three derivations are copied between scripts, and
# nothing notices when they drift). `dont_write_bytecode` because the import would otherwise leave
# a `helpers/__pycache__/` behind on every run, which is an untracked directory a gate has no
# business creating.
sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(root, "helpers"))
import name_census                                                          # noqa: E402
import name_rules                                                           # noqa: E402
import name_crossings                                                       # noqa: E402
import name_ruled                                                           # noqa: E402

# What carries a block, and which status it holds, is `helpers/name_census.py`'s, shared with
# `script/metrics`, which counts the same names out of each week's `git ls-tree`. What stays HERE is
# the sourcing: a gate is asked about the tree in front of it, so this passes every tracked or
# untracked-and-not-ignored file that exists on disk, and reads each one from disk. `helpers/` (the
# helper drawer) is out of scope; milestone 446 (the naming worklist says what it covers) priced and
# refused it, and design/naming/provenance-limits.md carries the reason.
#
# `package` is a PROVISIONAL name for the kind (an architect names things). It is Cargo's own word,
# which is the argument for it, and it is imprecise against Cargo's strict taxonomy, where a package
# contains crates and everything in `crates/` is one too. What the tree means by it is narrower and
# positional, the way the other kinds are: `crate` is a thing in `crates/`, `program` a thing in
# `components/src/` or `fixtures/src/`, `script` a thing in `script/`, and `package` a Cargo package
# that is none of those.
#
# **Why that kind had to exist** (calef, 2026-08-18). `script/names std_exerciser` answered "neither
# a name in the tree nor a recorded refusal" for weeks, because the three surfaces before it were
# the only places this tool looked. Eight packages live outside `crates/` and not one carried a
# provenance block, so the claim of milestone 115 (the names that were ratified, and the ones that
# were refused), that a name's provenance lives beside the name, was false for every one of them,
# silently. A registry with a hole is worse than no registry: it answers confidently about the names
# it happens to cover.
#
# Only an item or module that CARRIES a marker is tracked. Thousands of `pub fn`s have no record of
# why they are named so, and failing them would build the wall this script refuses to build. A
# `directory` is the exception: §75 already required the README, so a directory without one fails.
# Two stem families are not names anybody ratifies and are skipped: a stem that opens with a digit
# (a numbered or dated record) and everything under design/roadmap/, whose slugs restate milestone
# titles, which calef ruled drafts on 2026-08-04.


def tracked(*patterns, flags=()):
    out = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "ls-files", "-z", *flags, "--", *patterns],
                         capture_output=True, text=True, check=True).stdout
    return [p for p in out.split("\0") if p]


def read(rel):
    try:
        return open(os.path.join(root, rel), errors="replace").read()
    except (FileNotFoundError, IsADirectoryError):
        return None


files = [p for p in tracked(flags=("--cached", "--others", "--exclude-standard"))
         if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(root, p))]
records, problems, _missing, unblocked = name_census.census(files, read)

by_kind = {}
for r in records:
    by_kind.setdefault(r["kind"], []).append(r)

ratified = [r for r in records if r["status"] == "ratified"]
recorded = [r for r in records if r["status"] == "recorded"]
unrecorded = [r for r in records if r["status"] == "unrecorded"]
# `provisional` is a claim about INTENT (whoever chose this expects it to change), where the other
# three are claims about the RECORD. §89 added it on 2026-08-16, after milestone 117's second
# stranger run wrote the word AGENTS.md tells a lane to write and got a red gate for it: the file
# said `provisional` in four places and the gate knew three other words. The states are orthogonal
# in principle (a name can be recorded and still provisional) and this spends one word on the
# common case rather than modelling the cross product, which nothing has needed.
provisional = [r for r in records if r["status"] == "provisional"]
refusals = [(bad, r) for r in records for bad in r["refused"]]

# **A name calef has ruled and nobody has renamed is not a question for him** (helpers/name_ruled.py).
# It stays in `provisional` (the block says so until the rename lands) and in the worklist count;
# `--unratified` prints it in its own section, and the check below fails one left past the limit.
ruled_owed = name_ruled.owed(provisional)
try:
    _rulings = open(os.path.join(root, name_rules.RULES_DOC)).read()
except OSError:
    _rulings = ""
problems.extend(name_ruled.overdue(records, name_ruled.today_utc(), _rulings))

# **The worklist is every name that is not ratified, and it is computed once.** Every line below that
# says how much still wants an architect reads THIS list, because until 2026-09-19 three lines
# computed it
# three ways: `--check` printed `recorded + unrecorded` beside a pointer to `--unratified`, which
# printed `provisional + recorded + unrecorded`, and both census lines dropped `provisional`, so the
# three numbers a reader was shown did not sum to the total. Milestone 117's fourth stranger found it
# on 2026-08-18, by adding a program with a provisional name exactly as it had been told to and
# watching its own name vanish from the gate's count.
#
# **`provisional` belongs in it, and the reason is this script's own sort order**: `--unratified`
# puts provisional names FIRST in every tier, because their author has already said they are wrong
# and so they are the shortest conversation an architect can have (§89). A count that left them out
# under-reported exactly the part of the worklist worth reading first, and design/naming.md defines
# the worklist as what `--unratified` prints. So the two-category number was the bug and the
# three-category one was right.
worklist = provisional + recorded + unrecorded
KINDS = ["crate", "program", "package", "item", "module", "directory", "document", "script"]
PLURAL = {"directory": "directories"}

# The worklist's order, and it is exposure rather than alphabet or count, because exposure is what
# makes a wrong name expensive. A program is what a person types at the prompt, so it is read by
# everyone who uses the system; a crate is what a newcomer greps before opening anything (the naming
# tenet's own argument for putting crates in scope); a `script/` entry point is typed by whoever
# works ON the tree rather than in it.
#
# The tier is the KIND, deliberately, and not "programs a person actually types". That second split
# is the two-tier rule calef rejected on 2026-08-01, keyed on a property that is not stable: `wc`
# went from internal plumbing to a prompt-typed pipeline stage inside a day. Every program in
# `components/src/` and `fixtures/src/` is in the initial ramdisk and can be typed, so the kind is
# the honest tier and needs no
# classification anybody could get wrong.
EXPOSURE = {"program": 0, "crate": 1, "package": 2, "item": 3, "module": 3,
            "directory": 4, "document": 4, "script": 5}
# `package` sits between `crate` and `script`: a package name is read in a `[dependencies]`
# list and in a `use` statement, which is the crate tier, but most of these are structural
# (`kernel`, `user`, `xtask`) and a stranger meets them as directories rather than as choices.
# Within a tier, a name nobody can justify outranks one whose reasoning merely lacks a signature.
# `provisional` outranks both: its author has SAID it is wrong, so it is the shortest
# conversation an architect can have.
URGENCY = {"provisional": 0, "unrecorded": 1, "recorded": 2}

# A refused name that something in the tree still USES is a contradiction worth seeing: either the
# refusal was reversed and nobody said so, or a name came back. Reported, never fatal, because a
# refused word can legitimately survive as ordinary English (`brief`, `measure`) and a gate that
# fires on prose is a gate people learn to skip.
live = {r["name"] for r in records}
contradictions = sorted({bad for bad, _ in refusals if bad in live})

# ---- report ------------------------------------------------------------------
if mode == "--check":
    for p in sorted(set(problems)):
        print(f"names: {p}", file=sys.stderr)
    if problems:
        print("", file=sys.stderr)
        print("Every crate, program and script/ entry point carries a provenance block in its own "
              "header:", file=sys.stderr)
        print("  Name: ratified 2026-08-04 (calef, milestone 63). Refused `x` (why).",
              file=sys.stderr)
        print("  Name: recorded (milestone 46). <what the tree already argues, and where>",
              file=sys.stderr)
        print("  Name: unrecorded. <what the history does and does not say>", file=sys.stderr)
        print("Exactly one such block per file, spelled that way. A second block is unreachable "
              "(the parse stops at the first) and a", file=sys.stderr)
        print("bolded, indented or `///` one is invisible, which is how two of calef's "
              "ratifications sat on the worklist for a week.", file=sys.stderr)
        print("See design/naming.md. Presence is the gate, never `ratified`: a name waiting on an "
              "architect never fails a build.", file=sys.stderr)
        print("`unrecorded` is a real answer; a ratification you cannot cite is not.",
              file=sys.stderr)
        print("An item marks its name with a `/// Name: provisional, <who, when>.` paragraph, a "
              "documentation directory with a", file=sys.stderr)
        print("README paragraph opening `Name:`, a document with a paragraph opening `Name:` "
              "under its title; see the kinds in this script's header.", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(1)
    print(f"names: {len(records)} names carry provenance "
          f"({', '.join(f'{len(by_kind.get(k, []))} {PLURAL.get(k, k + chr(115))}' for k in KINDS)})")
    print(f"names: {len(ratified)} ratified, {len(provisional)} provisional, {len(recorded)} "
          f"recorded, {len(unrecorded)} unrecorded, {len(refusals)} refusals recorded beside them")
    print(f"names: {len(ruled_owed)} of the provisional are ruled with the rename owed "
          f"(fails past {name_ruled.MAX_AGE_DAYS} days)")
    print(f"names: {len(worklist)} still want an architect "
          f"(script/names --unratified), which is a worklist and not a failure")
    print(f"names: {len(unblocked)} modules, documents and pub items carry no block, held to a list that only shrinks "
          f"(helpers/module_block_ratchet.py)")
    legacy = [r for r in records if r.get("legacy")]
    if legacy:
        print(f"names: NOTE {len(legacy)} document blocks are still read from a README paragraph; "
              f"helpers/document_name_migrate.py --apply moves them into their documents",
              file=sys.stderr)
    for bad in contradictions:
        print(f"names: NOTE {bad!r} is recorded as refused and is also a live name", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(0)

if mode == "--refused":
    if not refusals:
        print("no refusals recorded")
        sys.exit(0)
    print(f"REFUSED ({len(refusals)}), and what holds each refusal\n")
    for bad, r in sorted(refusals, key=lambda t: t[0].lower()):
        print(f"  {bad:<28} {r['kind']} {r['name']}")
    print("\nscript/names <name> prints the reason.")
    sys.exit(0)

if mode == "--provisional":
    print(f"PROVISIONAL ({len(provisional)} of {len(records)}): the author expects these to change\n")
    for r in sorted(provisional, key=lambda r: (EXPOSURE[r["kind"]], r["name"])):
        print(f"  {r['kind']:<8} {r['name']:<28} {r['path']}")
    print(f"\n{len(provisional)} names their own author called wrong. The shortest conversation on "
          f"the worklist, which is why they sort first in --unratified.")
    sys.exit(0)

if mode == "--unrecorded":
    print(f"UNRECORDED ({len(unrecorded)} of {len(records)}): nothing outside the block says why\n")
    for r in sorted(unrecorded, key=lambda r: (r["kind"], r["name"])):
        print(f"  {r['kind']:<8} {r['name']}")
    print(f"\n{len(unrecorded)} of {len(records)} names have no reasoning anywhere in the tree. "
          f"See --unratified for these plus the {len(recorded)} that are argued but unsigned.")
    sys.exit(0)

def rank_crossings():
    """Set `r["crossing"]` on every item and module: the defining package and who reaches it.

    Run for `--unratified` only: it reads every tracked Rust file (about 2 s), and a single-name
    lookup should not pay for a ranking it does not print. `helpers/name_crossings.py` says what
    counts as crossing and what the measure gets wrong.
    """
    cands, by_key = [], {}
    for r in records:
        if r["kind"] not in ("item", "module") or r["status"] == "ratified":
            continue
        file, _, line = r["path"].partition(":")
        key = (r["kind"], r["name"], r["path"])
        by_key[key] = r
        cands.append((key, r["name"], file, r["what"] if r["kind"] == "item" else "module",
                      int(line) if line else 1))
    manifests = {os.path.dirname(p): open(os.path.join(root, p)).read()
                 for p in tracked("*Cargo.toml") if not p.startswith("vendor/")}
    sources = {p: open(os.path.join(root, p), errors="replace").read()
               for p in tracked("*.rs") if not p.startswith(("vendor/", "patches/"))}
    for r in records:
        r["crossing"] = None
    for key, hit in name_crossings.crossings(cands, manifests, sources).items():
        by_key[key]["crossing"] = hit


if mode == "--unratified":
    rank_crossings()

    def group(r):
        """0 and the defining package for a crossing name, 1 for an internal one; others 0."""
        if r["kind"] not in ("item", "module"):
            return 0, ""
        return (0, r["crossing"][0]) if r["crossing"] else (1, "")

    owed_ids = {id(r) for r in ruled_owed}
    todo = sorted((r for r in worklist if id(r) not in owed_ids),
                  key=lambda r: (EXPOSURE[r["kind"]], group(r), KINDS.index(r["kind"]),
                                 URGENCY[r["status"]],
                                 r["path"] if r["kind"] == "document" else r["name"]))
    if not worklist:
        print("every name in the tree is ratified")
        sys.exit(0)
    print(f"UNRATIFIED ({len(worklist)} of {len(records)}), in the order worth working through\n")
    if ruled_owed:
        today = name_ruled.today_utc()
        print(f"RULED, RENAME OWED ({len(ruled_owed)}): calef has ruled these, so do not ask him "
              f"again; perform the rename.")
        print(f"The check fails one left past {name_ruled.MAX_AGE_DAYS} days (design/naming.md).\n")
        for r in sorted(ruled_owed, key=lambda r: (r["ruled_on"], r["name"])):
            print(f"  {r['kind']:<8} {r['name']:<28} -> {r['ruled_to']:<28} ruled {r['ruled_on']} "
                  f"({name_ruled.age_days(r['ruled_on'], today)} days)")
        for r in name_ruled.unparsed(provisional):
            print(f"  NOT PARSED: {r['kind']} {r['name']} says \"and ruled\" in a shape "
                  f"helpers/name_ruled.py cannot read; it is listed below as plain provisional")
        print()
    print("Exposure first: a program is typed at the prompt, a crate is what a newcomer greps,")
    print("an item or module is read in a `use` line (one another package reaches first), a")
    print("directory or document is what a reader")
    print("opens, and a script/ entry point is typed by whoever works on the tree. Within a tier,")
    print("a name nobody can justify comes before one whose reasoning merely lacks a signature.\n")
    # design/naming.md is the authority for the conventions a ratification is judged against
    # (DECISIONS §155). AGENTS.md keeps only "names are calef's, ship provisional", so whoever is
    # about to rule is reading the wrong file unless something says so, and this is the command
    # they are already running.
    print("The conventions a name is judged against are design/naming.md, which is the rule.\n")
    tier = None
    for r in todo:
        if (r["kind"], r["status"], group(r)) != tier:
            tier = (r["kind"], r["status"], group(r))
            where = ""
            if r["kind"] in ("item", "module"):
                where = (f", crossing from {tier[2][1]}" if tier[2][0] == 0
                         else ", internal to one package")
            print(f"  {PLURAL.get(r['kind'], r['kind'] + 's')}, {r['status']}{where}")
        label = f"{r['name']} ({r['what']})" if r["kind"] == "item" else r["name"]
        if r.get("owner"):
            label = f"{r['name']} (of {r['owner']})"
        seen = ""
        if r.get("crossing"):
            users = r["crossing"][1]
            seen = "  <- " + ", ".join(users[:3]) + (f" +{len(users) - 3}" if len(users) > 3 else "")
        print(f"    {label:<40} {r['path']}{seen}")
    inner = [r for r in todo if r["kind"] in ("item", "module")]
    cross = [r for r in inner if r["crossing"]]
    print(f"\nitems and modules: {len(cross)} cross a package boundary "
          f"({sum(r['kind'] == 'item' for r in cross)} items, "
          f"{sum(r['kind'] == 'module' for r in cross)} modules), {len(inner) - len(cross)} are "
          f"internal. Crossing names are reviewed first (calef, 2026-10-17); internal ones "
          f"still need ratifying.")
    print(f"{len(ruled_owed)} ruled, rename owed (calef has answered), "
          f"{len(provisional) - len(ruled_owed)} provisional (the author expects a change), "
          f"{len(unrecorded)} unrecorded (research, then a ruling), "
          f"{len(recorded)} recorded (a ruling only).")
    print("script/names <name> prints what the history does and does not say about one of them.")
    sys.exit(0)

if mode and not mode.startswith("-"):
    hits = [r for r in records if r["name"] == mode]
    refused_hits = [(bad, r) for bad, r in refusals if bad == mode]
    if not hits and not refused_hits:
        # **A scoped answer, not a confident wrong one** (milestone 446, the naming worklist says
        # what it covers, and stops saying what it used to). `helpers/` is deliberately outside the
        # table, and nine of its seventeen files carry a `Name:` paragraph anyway. Left
        # alone, `script/names merge-drain` said "neither a name in the tree nor a recorded refusal"
        # about a file whose header argues the name at length, which is the exact shape the
        # `package` kind was added to fix one level along: a registry with a hole answering
        # confidently about the names it happens to cover. Naming the scope costs six lines and
        # sends the reader to the file instead of to nothing.
        helper = next((os.path.join("helpers", mode + ext)
                       for ext in (".sh", ".py")
                       if os.path.exists(os.path.join(root, "helpers", mode + ext))), None)
        if helper:
            print(f"names: {mode!r} is a helper under helpers/, which the naming worklist leaves out "
                  f"on purpose (design/naming.md's BUGS section says why).")
            print(f"       Its record, if it has one, is in {helper} rather than in this table.")
            sys.exit(1)
        # The two directories themselves are names too, and calef ratified one of them on
        # 2026-09-23. Neither is a crate, a program, a `script/` entry point or a package, so
        # nothing in the table above can carry the record and the answer above was the confident
        # wrong one this block exists to refuse. The record is the Scripts section of
        # design/naming/programs-scripts-and-directories.md.
        if mode in ("script", "helpers"):
            print(f"names: {mode!r} is a directory rather than one of the four kinds this table "
                  f"covers, so its record is prose.")
            print("       design/naming/programs-scripts-and-directories.md's Scripts section has it: "
                  "`helpers` was ratified by calef on 2026-09-23 (UTC), replacing `scripts`.")
            sys.exit(0)
        print(f"names: {mode!r} is neither a name in the tree nor a recorded refusal")
        sys.exit(1)
    for r in hits:
        print(f"{r['kind']} {r['name']}  ({r['path']})")
        if r.get("rule"):
            print(f"  ratified {r['date']} by rule ({r['rule']})")
        if r.get("record"):
            print(f"  recorded with its crate's names, outside the crate, at {r['record']}")
        print(f"  {r['text']}\n")
    for bad, r in refused_hits:
        print(f"REFUSED for {r['kind']} {r['name']}  ({r['path']})")
        print(f"  {r['text']}\n")
    sys.exit(0)

if mode:
    print(f"names: unknown argument {mode!r} (no argument, --unratified, --provisional, --refused, --unrecorded, "
          f"--check, or a name)", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(2)

for kind in KINDS:
    group = sorted(by_kind.get(kind, []), key=lambda r: r["name"])
    tally = {s: sum(1 for r in group if r["status"] == s)
             for s in ("ratified", "provisional", "recorded", "unrecorded")}
    print(f"\n{kind.upper()}S ({len(group)}: {tally['ratified']} ratified, "
          f"{tally['provisional']} provisional, {tally['recorded']} recorded, "
          f"{tally['unrecorded']} unrecorded)")
    for r in group:
        when = r["date"] if r["status"] == "ratified" else r["status"]
        tail = f"  refused {len(r['refused'])}" if r["refused"] else ""
        print(f"  {r['name']:<28} {when:<12}{tail}")

# The total counts a module or document with no block, which is in no status: each is a file whether
# or not anyone wrote its block (helpers/name_census.py), and script/metrics' `names_total` is this.
print(f"\ntotal: {len(records) + len(unblocked)} names, {len(ratified)} ratified, "
      f"{len(provisional)} provisional, {len(recorded)} recorded, {len(unrecorded)} unrecorded, "
      f"{len(unblocked)} with no block, {len(refusals)} refusals")
print(f"{len(worklist)} still want an architect: script/names --unratified")
if contradictions:
    print(f"refused but live: {', '.join(contradictions)}")
for p in sorted(set(problems)):
    print(f"PROBLEM: {p}")
PY
